tion of scleral proteoglycan synthesis during the recovery from induced myopia.</article-title><source>Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci</source><year>2007</year><volume>48</volume><fpage>2957</fpage><lpage>66</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17591860</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r21"><label>21</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nickla</surname><given-names>DL</given-names></name><name><surname>Wildsoet</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Wallman</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Compensation for spectacle lenses involves changes in proteoglycan synthesis in both the sclera and choroid. Erratum in: Curr Eye Res 1997 Jun;16(6):624-5.</article-title><source>Curr Eye Res</source><year>1997</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>320</fpage><lpage>6</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9134320</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r22"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>De Stefano</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name><name><surname>Mugnaini</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Fine structure of the choroidal coat of the avian eye. Lymphatic vessels. Comment in: Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci. 1998 Apr;39(5):854-5.</article-title><source>Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci</source><year>1997</year><volume>38</volume><fpage>1241</fpage><lpage>60</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9152244</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="r23"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Poukens</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Glasgow</surname><given-names>BJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Demer</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Nonvascular contractile cells in sclera and choroid of humans and monkeys.</article-title><source>Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci</s